  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 AM MDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nWe have now reached the end of the ninth month of the year, about \n75 percent through the year (74.5 percent to be exact). And we are \nnow in, as a rule, on of the nicest times of year in Wyoming. This \ngenerally runs from after Labor Day through the middle of October. \nYes, I realize we have had some snowstorms, and some substantial \nones during this time frame, but most days are nice. And most of the \nnext several days will be no exception.\n\nFor most of the area today, the weather will be described by a \ncouple of songs popular around the turn of the millennium. The better \nknown one is by U2, the lesser known one, at least on this side of \nthe Atlantic, is by the British band 3 Colours Red. And those songs \nare \"Beautiful Day\" and \"A Beautiful Day\", respectively. Today will \nfeature a cool morning, a comfortably warm afternoon and a good \ndeal of sunshine. As for cloud cover, the most would be across the \nfar north, and in southern Wyoming, where some high level moisture \nmay move up from the remnants of Hurricane Polo. However, showers \nwill remain to the south and east of our area. It could become \nbreezy late today across portions of northern Wyoming, but nothing \nby Wyoming standards. So, if you can, get out and enjoy it.\n\nA weak cold front will make a run at Wyoming Thursday into Thursday \nnight. Impacts from it look minimal at this time. Guidance has \ntrended further north with the jet forcing than at this time \nyesterday. The front also is of continental origin and has little \nmoisture to work with. As a result, the chance of showers Thursday \ninto Thursday night has also dropped. There is still a small chance \nof shower Thursday night, but it is 1 out of 6 at the most and \nlargely limited to north of a Lovell to Buffalo line. The main \nimpact we will be somewhat breezier conditions and a small reduction \nin temperatures, but only 5 to 10 degrees with the biggest drop in \nthe northern Bighorn Basin and Johnson County.\n\nFriday at this point looks like a mild and quiet day. Another cold \nfront will make a run at northern Wyoming Friday night into \nSaturday. However, the same criteria applies to this front as the \nlast one. The chance of showers is minimal and largely restricted to \nfar northeastern portions of the area. Again, the main impacts will \nbe for somewhat cooler temperatures East of the Divide and breezy \nconditions in the favored cold advection locations like the northern \nBig Horn Basin and Johnson County. \n\nIn the longer term, ridging will set up across much of the western \nUnited States and bring a period of mainly dry conditions and above \nnormal temperatures from Sunday through much of next week. There \nwill be another fast moving system moving west to east into the area \nMonday night into Tuesday. It could bring some showers during this \ntime frame. The main impact may be some increasing wind ahead of it. \nThis, combined with low humidity and unseasonably warm temperatures, \nmay bring elevated fire weather Monday afternoon to portions of the \narea. Otherwise, things look relatively uneventful next week with \nsome, shall I say, beautiful days a very good possibility.",
